            <ArticleTitle>The Stem Cell Hard Sell: Report from a Clinic's Patient Recruitment Seminar.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>14-16</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5966/sctm.2016-0208</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The growing direct-to-consumer, stem cell clinic industry in the U.S. uses a number of strategies for patient recruitment, including self-styled educational seminars, which may reach thousands of members of the public annually. Here I report on a first-hand experience at such a seminar that I recently attended. Numerous specific medical claims were made at the seminar: no potential for rejection; no side effects, including no pain; proven efficacy for a variety of conditions, including in particular arthritis and pain; and U.S. Food and Drug Administration approval. I discuss the potential impact of these kinds of seminars on the public and on the stem cell field. Stem Cells Translational Medicine 2017;6:14-16.</AbstractText>
